<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <!--
        ONE PERMISSION, AND ONLY ONE.

        RECORD_AUDIO, for voice notes, requested the first time someone taps
        record rather than at first launch (UX-FLOWS.md flow 1).

        Note what is NOT here, because it is the interesting part. Photos need
        no CAMERA permission: the *camera app* takes the picture and we hand it
        a FileProvider URI to write into. Gallery and document import need no
        storage permission: the Storage Access Framework is built so that the
        user choosing a file in the system picker IS the grant. Saving a backup
        needs none either, for the same reason.

        So the whole of voice, photos, documents and backup costs exactly one
        line in this file. Everything said below about INTERNET still holds and
        tool/verify_no_internet.sh still proves it against a built APK.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.RECORD_AUDIO" />

    <!--
        These two arrived on their own, and that is why they are written here.

        `androidx.biometric` merges them into the final manifest whether or not
        anyone asks. `tool/verify_no_internet.sh` reads permissions out of the
        BUILT APK rather than out of this file precisely so that cannot happen
        quietly — it failed the build, which is the script doing its job, and
        the fix is to declare them deliberately rather than to widen the script.

        Both are `protectionLevel="normal"`: granted at install, no runtime
        prompt, and they do not read a fingerprint — they only permit showing
        the system's own authentication dialog. The actual protection is that
        the keystore key is bound to BIOMETRIC_STRONG and the hardware refuses
        to use it otherwise. See BiometricVault.kt.

        USE_FINGERPRINT is the pre-Android-9 spelling and is deprecated; the
        library still declares it for older devices.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.USE_BIOMETRIC" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.USE_FINGERPRINT" />

    <!--
        THE REMINDER, AND THE TWO PERMISSIONS IT COSTS.

        Both are `protectionLevel="normal"` or a runtime prompt the user answers
        once, and neither grants access to anything. They are here because the
        owner asked for an optional daily nudge to write, and because a feature
        that silently stops working after a reboot is worse than no feature.

          POST_NOTIFICATIONS     Android 13+ runtime permission. Asked for at
                                 the moment somebody turns the reminder ON, and
                                 never at launch. Declining it turns the switch
                                 back off with an explanation; nothing else in
                                 the app is affected.

          RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED A repeating alarm does not survive a restart.
                                 Without this the reminder stops the first time
                                 the phone is turned off, for exactly the person
                                 who was relying on it. It grants no access to
                                 anything — it is a wake-up call.

        Deliberately NOT here: SCHEDULE_EXACT_ALARM. The reminder is inexact so
        Android can batch it, which costs the battery nothing and avoids a
        permission that Google reviews and that users see in the store listing.
        Nobody needs a journal prompt at 21:00:00 rather than 21:04.

        The reminder runs in a BroadcastReceiver with no keys, no database and
        no Flutter engine, so it is *incapable* of saying anything about what is
        in the vault. See Reminders.kt.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED" />

    <!--
        ONE PERMISSION, AND NO INTERNET. Both halves matter.

        This file said "NO <uses-permission> ENTRIES. NOT ONE." until voice
        notes were built, and RECORD_AUDIO above is the honest amendment. It is
        recorded here rather than quietly replaced, because the claim a user can
        verify has changed and pretending otherwise would be the beginning of
        the rot this comment exists to prevent.

        What has NOT changed is the claim that actually carries the threat
        model: **there is no INTERNET permission**, so a release build has no
        way to open a socket, and a stranger can confirm it in thirty seconds
        with `aapt dump permissions` on the APK. 02-security/THREAT-MODEL.md
        adversary #4 is us — the developer — and the answer there is not "we
        promise not to". A microphone permission on an app that provably cannot
        reach the network is a microphone that records to your own disk.

        The debug and profile manifests DO declare INTERNET, because the Flutter
        tool needs it for hot reload and for driving on-device tests. Those
        variants never ship. `tool/verify_no_internet.*` proves the release
        artifact stays clean; do not weaken it.

        WHAT IS DELIBERATELY STILL ABSENT, AND HOW:

          CAMERA          — not needed. The *camera app* takes the photograph;
                            we hand it a FileProvider URI to write into.
          READ_MEDIA_*    — not needed. Gallery import goes through the Storage
          READ_EXTERNAL     Access Framework, where the user picking a file in
          WRITE_EXTERNAL    the system's own picker IS the grant. Saving and
                            restoring backups work the same way.

        So the whole of photos, documents and backup cost nothing here. That is
        not a coincidence — SAF was chosen over the usual plugins for exactly
        this reason. Before adding any permission, re-read THREAT-MODEL.md: one
        added for a "small" feature costs a claim that took the whole
        architecture to earn.
    -->

    <application
        android:label="${appLabel}"
        android:name="${applicationName}"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"

        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:dataExtractionRules="@xml/data_extraction_rules"
        android:fullBackupContent="@xml/backup_rules"

        android:enableOnBackInvokedCallback="true"

        android:networkSecurityConfig="@xml/network_security_config"

        android:allowClearUserData="true"

        android:hasFragileUserData="true"

        android:memtagMode="async">

        <!--
            ONE WAY UP. ISSUE 5.

            *"The app has just one mode - portrait mode - it doesn't rotate if
            the screen is rotated, and also it doesn't rotate even if the phone
            has auto rotate turned on! Everything stays in one mode."*

            `android:screenOrientation="portrait"` below is the authoritative
            half of that. The window manager reads it before Flutter exists, so
            the first frame is upright and so is the recents thumbnail, and it
            overrules the phone's own auto-rotate switch - which a Dart-side
            preference alone does not do reliably on every OEM build. The other
            half is SystemChrome.setPreferredOrientations in main(); see
            lib/core/platform/orientation.dart for why neither is enough alone.

            "portrait" rather than "userPortrait": userPortrait would still
            permit upside-down on a handset that allows it, and upside-down is
            the rotation nobody ever asks for on purpose.

            `configChanges` still lists orientation. It is now unreachable for
            rotation and is left alone because it also covers density, locale
            and font scale, which do still change under a running activity.

            ══ ROUND FIFTEEN, ISSUE 12 - AND THIS IS NO LONGER THE WHOLE
            STORY ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════

            "When the window is resized - when using split screen or floating
            screen - I want you to set that the app is responsive then too!"

            Two rules now override `screenOrientation` below, and both of them
            apply to the tablet this app is judged on:

              * In MULTI-WINDOW mode Android has always ignored an activity's
                orientation request. Split screen can be short and wide
                whatever this file asks for.

              * Since ANDROID 16, for an app targeting SDK 36 - which this one
                does - the system also ignores orientation, resizability and
                aspect-ratio restrictions on any display whose smallest width
                is 600dp or more. The Redmi Pad is 686dp.

            So on his own device the app is already fully rotatable and
            resizable, and nothing that can be written here will bring the
            round-five behaviour back. `orientation.dart` used to say there was
            "no moment and no code path where the app is anything but upright";
            that sentence is corrected there.

            The attribute stays because it is still the truth on a phone, which
            is where he originally asked for it, and because losing it would
            change behaviour on every handset for the sake of a device where it
            is already inert.

            `resizeableActivity` is declared rather than left to its default.
            The default is true for every targetSdk this app has ever had, so
            this changes nothing today - it is here so that a future default,
            or an OEM's own idea of what a journal app wants, cannot quietly
            letterbox the thing. `test/widget/resizing_test.dart` walks a live
            window through split screen, a floating window and landscape, and
            `responsive_test.dart` builds every screen at those sizes.
        -->
        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:launchMode="singleTop"
            android:screenOrientation="portrait"
            android:resizeableActivity="true"
            android:taskAffinity=""
            android:excludeFromRecents="false"
            android:theme="@style/LaunchTheme"
            android:configChanges="orientation|keyboardHidden|keyboard|screenSize|smallestScreenSize|locale|layoutDirection|fontScale|screenLayout|density|uiMode"
            android:hardwareAccelerated="true"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize">

            <!-- Specifies an Android theme to apply to this Activity as soon as
                 the Android process has started. This theme is visible to the user
                 while the Flutter UI initializes. After that, this theme continues
                 to determine the Window background behind the Flutter UI. -->
            <meta-data
              android:name="io.flutter.embedding.android.NormalTheme"
              android:resource="@style/NormalTheme"
              />

            <!--
                SHARING INTO LAMPLIGHT. ISSUE 13.

                *"A mobile - android, ios, tablet - has other apps: gallery,
                mail, files, messenger, WhatsApp, telegram, Instagram. These
                apps has texts, photos, videos and other documents, which can be
                shared, right? Make it possible that those things are allowed to
                be shared to Lamplight, so it can be saved there - on that day,
                that time."*

                THIS IS AN INBOUND DOOR, AND IT IS WORTH BEING PRECISE ABOUT
                WHAT IT DOES AND DOES NOT OPEN.

                What it does: puts Lamplight in the system share sheet, so the
                user - and only the user, by tapping Lamplight in that sheet -
                can hand this app a file or some text. Android then grants us a
                read permission on that one item, for that one intent.

                What it does NOT do:
                  - it grants no app the ability to read anything of ours. An
                    intent filter is a doorway *in*. Nothing here is exported
                    for reading, and the FileProvider below is still
                    exported="false".
                  - it adds no permission. Receiving a content:// URI through a
                    share is the grant, exactly as the Storage Access Framework
                    is the grant for the pickers. The manifest gains no
                    <uses-permission> line for any of this.
                  - it does not touch INTERNET. tool/verify_no_internet.sh still
                    passes against the release APK.

                The one real consideration is that another app can *start* this
                activity. It already could - it carries LAUNCHER through its
                aliases - and the app opens locked, so what a hostile sender
                reaches is the passcode screen. The shared item is held
                unopened until the vault is unlocked; see Sharing.kt.

                mimeType="*/*" on purpose. He listed csv, rtf, pdf, apk, heic,
                gif, svg, vcf, wav, m4a, mov, mkv, avi, zip, rar, 7z, opus, ogg,
                epub, html, json, docx, xlsx, pptx, jpg, png and webp in ISSUE
                12, and enumerating them here would be a list to keep in sync
                with the one in the code, forever, wrongly. Anything can be
                *kept*; what can be *opened* is ISSUE 12's question and is
                answered in document_viewer.dart.
            -->
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.SEND" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
                <data android:mimeType="*/*" />
            </intent-filter>

            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.SEND_MULTIPLE" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
                <data android:mimeType="*/*" />
            </intent-filter>

        </activity>

        <!--
            THE LAUNCHER ICON, IN TWO FLAVOURS.

            MainActivity itself no longer carries the LAUNCHER filter. These two
            aliases do, one at a time, and swapping which one is enabled is the
            only way Android lets an app change its own icon. See IconSwitcher.kt
            for the ordering rule that keeps the home-screen shortcut alive
            through the swap.

            The Dark one is enabled at install because dark is the app.s default
            theme. Anybody on light gets the swap the first time they open it.
        -->
        <activity-alias
            android:name="com.probablypiyush.lamplight.Dark"
            android:targetActivity=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:enabled="true"
            android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
            android:label="Lamplight">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER"/>
            </intent-filter>
        </activity-alias>

        <activity-alias
            android:name="com.probablypiyush.lamplight.Light"
            android:targetActivity=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:enabled="false"
            android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_light"
            android:label="Lamplight">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER"/>
            </intent-filter>
        </activity-alias>

        <!--
            THE OTHER FIVE ACCENTS. ISSUE 6b.

            *"The request is that the icon's light takes the colour of the
            chosen accent"* — he showed amber against purple.

            A launcher icon is a manifest attribute, read by the launcher and
            fixed at install time; there is no runtime tint. So an accent that
            reaches the home screen has to be its own alias, and six accents in
            two themes is twelve of them. The two above are Amber, the default,
            and keep the original drawable names so a phone that has never been
            near this feature is unaffected.

            All ten below are disabled at install. IconSwitcher.kt enables
            exactly one, and its ordering rule — enable the new one *before*
            disabling the others — is what stops the home-screen shortcut
            vanishing during the swap. That rule matters more with twelve
            components than it did with two.

            Only the lamp's **light** differs between them. The shade stays the
            palette's own ink, because the shade is the object and the light is
            the accent — six marks under six lamps rather than six marks.
        -->

        <activity-alias
            android:name="com.probablypiyush.lamplight.Rose"
            android:targetActivity=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:enabled="false"
            android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_rose"
            android:label="Lamplight">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER"/>
            </intent-filter>
        </activity-alias>

        <activity-alias
            android:name="com.probablypiyush.lamplight.RoseLight"
            android:targetActivity=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:enabled="false"
            android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_rose_light"
            android:label="Lamplight">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER"/>
            </intent-filter>
        </activity-alias>

        <activity-alias
            android:name="com.probablypiyush.lamplight.Sage"
            android:targetActivity=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:enabled="false"
            android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_sage"
            android:label="Lamplight">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER"/>
            </intent-filter>
        </activity-alias>

        <activity-alias
            android:name="com.probablypiyush.lamplight.SageLight"
            android:targetActivity=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:enabled="false"
            android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_sage_light"
            android:label="Lamplight">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER"/>
            </intent-filter>
        </activity-alias>

        <activity-alias
            android:name="com.probablypiyush.lamplight.Slate"
            android:targetActivity=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:enabled="false"
            android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_slate"
            android:label="Lamplight">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER"/>
            </intent-filter>
        </activity-alias>

        <activity-alias
            android:name="com.probablypiyush.lamplight.SlateLight"
            android:targetActivity=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:enabled="false"
            android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_slate_light"
            android:label="Lamplight">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER"/>
            </intent-filter>
        </activity-alias>

        <activity-alias
            android:name="com.probablypiyush.lamplight.Plum"
            android:targetActivity=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:enabled="false"
            android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_plum"
            android:label="Lamplight">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER"/>
            </intent-filter>
        </activity-alias>

        <activity-alias
            android:name="com.probablypiyush.lamplight.PlumLight"
            android:targetActivity=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:enabled="false"
            android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_plum_light"
            android:label="Lamplight">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER"/>
            </intent-filter>
        </activity-alias>

        <activity-alias
            android:name="com.probablypiyush.lamplight.Ember"
            android:targetActivity=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:enabled="false"
            android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_ember"
            android:label="Lamplight">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER"/>
            </intent-filter>
        </activity-alias>

        <activity-alias
            android:name="com.probablypiyush.lamplight.EmberLight"
            android:targetActivity=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:enabled="false"
            android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_ember_light"
            android:label="Lamplight">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER"/>
            </intent-filter>
        </activity-alias>

        <!--
            The camera app writes a photo into our own cache through this, and
            nothing else uses it. `exported="false"` plus `grantUriPermissions`
            means no app can reach in on its own — each URI is granted for one
            write and expires with the intent. See res/xml/file_paths.xml.
        -->
        <provider
            android:name="androidx.core.content.FileProvider"
            android:authorities="${applicationId}.captures"
            android:exported="false"
            android:grantUriPermissions="true">
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.support.FILE_PROVIDER_PATHS"
                android:resource="@xml/file_paths" />
        </provider>

        <!--
            THE REMINDER'S TWO RECEIVERS.

            Both `exported="false"`, which is the important attribute on this
            whole page: an exported receiver is a doorway any app on the phone
            can knock on. Neither of these needs to be reachable from outside —
            one is woken by our own alarm, the other by the system's boot
            broadcast — so neither is.

            The boot receiver is `directBootAware="false"` by design. It should
            not run before the phone is unlocked for the first time, because
            there is nothing for it to do until there is a user.
        -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".ReminderReceiver"
            android:exported="false" />

        <receiver
            android:name=".BootReceiver"
            android:exported="false"
            android:directBootAware="false">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.BOOT_COMPLETED" />
            </intent-filter>
        </receiver>

        <!-- Don't delete the meta-data below.
             This is used by the Flutter tool to generate GeneratedPluginRegistrant.java -->
        <meta-data
            android:name="flutterEmbedding"
            android:value="2" />
    </application>

    <!--
        Flutter's template puts a <queries> block here declaring PROCESS_TEXT,
        which lets the engine enumerate other installed apps that can act on
        selected text, and adds them to the text-selection toolbar.

        Removed on purpose. In a journal, that toolbar would offer to hand a
        selected sentence to an arbitrary third-party app — one tap, no
        confirmation, straight out of the vault. It also lets us fingerprint
        which apps are installed, which is information we have no business
        having. Losing it costs nothing: copy, paste and share still work.

        ROUND EIGHT, ISSUE 1C - this decision has a consequence, and it was
        found the expensive way. Package visibility means resolveActivity()
        returns null for an intent the app is not allowed to see, which is
        indistinguishable from "nothing can open this". HandOff used to gate
        "open with" on exactly that call, so the feature reported that the
        phone had nothing to open a PDF with - on every phone, for every file.

        The fix was to delete the check rather than to add a <queries> block,
        because the chooser resolves on its own and Android says so itself when
        nothing can handle a file. See HandOff.kt for the full argument.

        So: still no <queries>. If a later phase genuinely needs an intent
        query, add it narrowly and say why here - and read HandOff.kt first,
        because the last thing that appeared to need one did not.
    -->

</manifest>
